{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case Three.",
"start": 0.75,
"end": 1.753,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Dry Raincoat.",
"start": 2.3529999999999998,
"end": 3.569,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "Tamsin Trevelyan's bookshop sat halfway up the main street of Tidewhistle Cove, and its proudest treasure lived in a glass case at the very back. It was a signed first copy of The Puffin Who Wouldn't Fly, the most famous book ever written about the village.",
"start": 4.969,
"end": 20.649,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "At five to three, Tamsin unlocked the case to dust it, and was called to the front of the shop by the telephone. She left the case unlocked. At three o'clock exactly the sky turned black, and a sudden downpour began. It was so heavy that the gutters overflowed and the main street ran like a river. It lasted twenty minutes and then stopped as quickly as it had started.",
"start": 21.499,
"end": 44.432,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "At twenty past three, Tamsin walked to the back of the shop. The case was open, and the book was gone.",
"start": 45.282,
"end": 51.426,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes had been next door in the chemist's, and Tamsin called her in.",
"start": 52.276,
"end": 56.031,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Three people were in or near the shop. Hedley Truscott was at the counter, dripping all over the floor. He had come in soaking wet at ten past three, and Tamsin said he had stood at the counter talking to her about fishing books from the moment he walked in. He had not been near the back.",
"start": 56.881,
"end": 74.246,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Young Pip Carew had been waiting under the shop's striped awning outside since a quarter to three, and he had sheltered there through the whole downpour. Tamsin could see him through the front window the entire time, and he had never come inside.",
"start": 75.096,
"end": 89.219,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The little brass bell over the shop door rings whenever anybody comes in or goes out. Between five to three and twenty past three, Tamsin said, it had rung only once, when Hedley came in.",
"start": 90.069,
"end": 102.25,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The third person was Wenna Polglaze, who was standing by the poetry shelves near the back.",
"start": 103.1,
"end": 108.327,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"What a day,\" said Wenna, shaking her head. \"I've only this minute run in from the harbour. Got caught in that rain without my umbrella. Silly me.\"",
"start": 109.177,
"end": 117.71,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes looked at Wenna's coat. It was dry. So was her hair. So were her shoes.",
"start": 118.56,
"end": 123.253,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? Who took the signed book, and what gave them away?",
"start": 124.353,
"end": 127.873,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 129.073,
"end": 130.097,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 130.997,
"end": 136.224,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 140.724,
"end": 141.748,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Wenna Polglaze took the book.",
"start": 142.848,
"end": 144.555,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "She said she had just run up from the harbour without an umbrella. But the downpour had been so heavy that Hedley was drenched after a short walk, and the street had been running like a river. If Wenna had really been outside, she would have been soaked to the skin. Her coat, hair and shoes were completely dry, so she must have been inside the shop since before three o'clock, when the rain began.",
"start": 145.405,
"end": 167.826,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The door bell had rung only once, for Hedley, so nobody else had come in or gone out after the case was unlocked. Hedley had stood at the counter in full view of Tamsin from the moment he arrived, and Pip had stayed outside under the awning the whole time. Only Wenna had been inside, at the back of the shop near the open case.",
"start": 168.676,
"end": 189.241,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The book was in Wenna's tote bag, wrapped in a scarf. She explained that her nephew adored the story, and she could never afford a copy like that. Tamsin, who is kind as well as clever, found her a lovely ordinary copy at a very small price, and the signed one went back into its case, locked this time.",
"start": 190.091,
"end": 209.206,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
